    {
      "abstract": "This study proposes a hybrid integrated method for floating offshore wind turbines (FOWTs) incorporating nonlinear hydrodynamic load modeling. The method establishes a hybrid nonlinear wave field–structure coupling framework by integrating the fully nonlinear incident wave field generated by OceanWave3D into OpenFAST. To enhance the prediction accuracy of nonlinear wave-induced responses, the nonlinear Froude-Krylov forces are evaluated from the precomputed wave pressure field and integrated over the dynamically reconstructed wetted surface. Hydrostatic restoring forces are corrected based on the instantaneous center of buoyancy, while diffraction and radiation contributions from potential-flow theory are scaled in proportion to the instantaneous wetted volume to accurately capture motion-induced variations in the hydrodynamic loading. The proposed integrated method is validated against experimental data from the WINDMOOR project, including free-decay and irregular wave tests, demonstrating good agreement in platform motions and mooring line tensions. Compared with conventional linear potential-flow tools, the hybrid approach significantly improves the prediction accuracy of low-frequency platform motions and mooring line tensions under irregular sea states. The hybrid integrated method provides a robust and computationally efficient tool for nonlinear analysis and design assessment of floating wind turbine systems.",
