    {
      "abstract": "High-fidelity wave-basin testing of floating wind turbines requires real-time reproduction of motion-dependent aerodynamic loads, but existing real-time hybrid model testing (RTHM) systems remain constrained by online aerodynamic-solver blocking and limited multi-axis load actuation. This study proposes an AI-enabled RTHM framework in which an OpenFAST-informed aerodynamic surrogate is embedded as an active numerical substructure and integrated with asynchronous command execution and multi-channel physical loading. A CNN-BiLSTM-Attention surrogate is trained using fully coupled OpenFAST data to predict one-step-ahead rotor thrust Fx and aerodynamic moments Mx, My, and Mz from turbulent wind input, turbine operating states, and real-time six-degree-of-freedom platform-motion feedback. A prediction–cache–release strategy decouples command release from AI inference, load scaling, and command generation, with the measured cache-release latency below 1 ms. The predicted loads are scaled and physically reproduced by a six-rotor aerodynamic loading device in a 1:85 wave-basin test of a 16 MW tension-leg platform floating wind turbine. The AI-predicted loads agree closely with OpenFAST references, with the minimum Q-Q coefficient of determination reaching 0.997. The corresponding measured thrust and moment channels achieve minimum Q-Q coefficients of 0.988 and 0.927, respectively. At the system-response level, the main statistical errors of platform surge, heave, pitch, and upwind center tendon tension remain below 9.6%. The proposed framework establishes a motion-feedback-in-the-loop AI numerical-substructure approach for real-time hybrid testing of the investigated 16 MW TLP floating wind turbine.",
